body:
**[Major]** `url.template` metric label can carry user-chosen resource IDs, risking unbounded cardinality.

`TemplatePath` relies on shape detection (`prefix_uppercaseOrDigits`) to identify Stripe-generated IDs. But user-chosen IDs on endpoints like `/v1/coupons/summer` or `/v1/products/my-product` don't match the shape and pass through untemplated. This means `url.template` becomes unbounded for those endpoints, violating the contract in `stripe.yaml` ("never by paths that embed resource IDs").

Consider adding a guard in `MetricAttributes` so the raw path never leaks into metric labels:

```go
if tmpl := TemplatePath(req.Path); tmpl != "" {
    // Only use the template as a metric label when it differs from the
    // raw path, i.e. at least one segment was replaced. When the template
    // equals the raw path, omit it to avoid user-chosen IDs becoming
    // unbounded label values.
    if tmpl != strings.TrimSpace(req.Path) || !strings.Contains(tmpl, IDPlaceholder) {
        attrs = append(attrs, semconv.URLTemplate(tmpl))
    }
}
```

Alternatively, this is an acknowledged known limitation — if so, consider adding an explicit note in the metric section of `stripe.yaml` about the cardinality gap for user-chosen IDs.

**[Major]** The `req == nil` and `req.URL == nil` guard branches are untested in both v81 and v82 `hook_test.go`.

These are new executable paths that should have unit tests verifying they are no-ops (no panic, no span created, no param rewritten). Consider adding:

```go
func TestBeforeRequest_NilRequestIsNoop(t *testing.T) {
    sr, _ := setupTestProviders(t)
    ictx := newCallContext(nil)
    require.NotPanics(t, func() {
        BeforeRequest(ictx, nil, nil, nil, nil)
    })
    assert.Empty(t, sr.Ended())
}

func TestBeforeRequest_NilURLIsNoop(t *testing.T) {
    sr, _ := setupTestProviders(t)
    req := &http.Request{}
    ictx := newCallContext(req)
    require.NotPanics(t, func() {
        BeforeRequest(ictx, nil, req, nil, nil)
    })
    assert.Same(t, req, ictx.GetParam(reqParamIndex))
    assert.Empty(t, sr.Ended())
}
```

