body:
The `opts.AutoPin` branch here can't execute in production. `applyOtelYAMLValidation` is only reachable through `processOtelYAMLFiles`, whose one production call site (`processPinConfigs`, guarded by `if !opts.AutoPin`) never invokes it when `AutoPin` is true — when AutoPin actually runs, YAML-bearing configs go through `materializeModuleConfigs` + `updatePinnedProjects` instead, not through this function.

The only thing that exercises this branch is `TestProcessOtelYAMLFiles_AutoPin`, which calls `processOtelYAMLFiles` directly with `PinOptions{AutoPin: true}` — bypassing the orchestrator that would otherwise never produce that combination. That gives false confidence: the test passes, but nothing in `Pin`/`AutoPin`/`pinLocked` can reach this code path.

Worth deciding: either this was meant to be wired up as an alternate AutoPin path and the routing in `processPinConfigs` should call it, or it's leftover from an earlier design and should go. If the latter, removing the branch simplifies this to a single unconditional prune-on-write:

```suggestion
		if opts.Prune {
			if writeErr := writeOtelYAMLImports(
				otelYAMLFiles[moduleDir], yamlImportsByModule[moduleDir],
			); writeErr != nil {
				return writeErr
			}
		}
```

`TestProcessOtelYAMLFiles_AutoPin` would need to go or be rewritten to call the function the way production code actually does, so it stops asserting behavior no caller can trigger.

